ml`, `_rels/.rels`, `xl/workbook.xml`, `xl/_rels/workbook.xml.rels`, `xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml`, `xl/theme/theme1.xml`, `xl/styles.xml`, `xl/sharedStrings.xml`, `xl/worksheets/_rels/sheet1.xml.rels`, `xl/printerSettings/printerSettings1.bin`, `docProps/core.xml`, `docProps/app.xml`\n\nApproximate uncompressed sizes from the local headers (bit-mangled, so read as ~): `xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml` ≈ 3 KB, `xl/sharedStrings.xml` ≈ 550 B. So the sheet is small — a handful of rows/labels — but its bytes are irrecoverable here. The presence of `sharedStrings.xml` means the text labels (\"Reaction N\", units, etc.) live in that string table, and numeric values are inline in `sheet1.xml` — both compressed.\n\nNote on `print`: I did not parse cell values, so there are no headers, no notes, and no numbers/units to dump verbatim — no partial credit table, no guesses.\n\n## For context (not from the file)\nSession logs show this xlsx is the attachment for the GAIA question *\"In the NIH translation of the original 1913 Michaelis-Menten Paper, what is the velocity of a reaction to four decimal places using the final equation in the paper based on the information for Reaction 7 in the Excel file?\"* So the sheet holds a short table of reaction parameters including a row/label \"Reaction 7\" — that is inference from the question text, **not** observed content.\n\n## To get the verbatim grid (needs bash permission, then run as-is)\n```bash\npython -c \"import openpyxl;wb=openpyxl.load_workbook(r'D:\\GoatCode-ts\\gaia\\data\\c526d8d6-5987-4da9-b24c-83466fa172f3.xlsx');[print('##',ws.title) or [print(' | '.join('' if c.value is None else repr(c.value) for c in row)) for row in ws.iter_rows()] for ws in wb.worksheets]\"\n```\n\n**Summary:** bash, webfetch, websearch and screenshot are all permission-blocked, and the `read` tool dumps the xlsx as lossy raw zip bytes whose deflate streams cannot be hand-decoded, so I recovered the archive's structure (1 sheet, shared-strings table present)